      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "We apply the Inclusion-Exclusion Principle, first, to determine how many ways we can color a single hexagon such that no two share the same color. We have three colors, and for any given hexagon, there are $3$ choices for its color. This gives us a total of $3^6 \\cdot 3$ ways to color a single hexagon, because the remaining $5$ vertices can be colored in $3^5$ ways.\n\nHowever, this initial count includes cases where one or more hexagons share an adjacent vertex with a different color. We need to subtract these cases from our total. To find how many of these cases occur, we consider three scenarios:\n\n1. All six hexagons are one color.\n2. Exactly two hexagons are of one color, and the other four are of another color.\n3. No two hexagons share an adjacent vertex with the same color.\n\nFor the first scenario, there are $3$ colors, and for each color, there is exactly one way to color the hexagons. Hence, there are only $3$ ways to color the hexagons entirely one color.\n\nFor the second scenario, we choose $2$ out of $6$ hexagons to be one color, and the remaining $4$ will be another color. There are $\\binom{6}{2}=15$ ways to choose $2$ hexagons for one color, and for each choice of $2$ hexagons, there are $3$ ways to choose the color for them. The remaining $4$ hexagons can be any of the $2$ remaining colors, giving us $2^4=16$ ways to color them. Thus, there are $15 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 16 = 720$ ways for this scenario.\n\nFor the third scenario, we use the fact that with the condition that no two neighboring hexagons share the same color, we cannot have all hexagons the same color. Therefore, we cannot have any three hexagons of the same color. Constraining ourselves to $3$ hexagons sharing a common color leaves us with $3$ hexagons of one color, $3$ of another, and $6-3=3$ remaining hexagons. Since one configuration (full green) has already been excluded, and at least two hexagons of each color must appear, there are $\\binom{4}{2}=6$ ways to partition $4$ hexagons into two groups of $2$ hexagons of the same color. For each of these, the grouped hexagons can be colored in $3$ ways. For the remaining hexagons, the colors are determined by their shared configurations, so there are $3$ ways to color these hexagons. Thus, the total number of ways to color the hexagons with adjacent vertices forbidden, but constrained by two hexagons of the same color, is $6 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3=54$.\n\nAdding the counts for scenarios two and three back into our total, we sum $3+720+54=777$ ways.\n\nHowever, upon re-evaluation, we realize that our earlier count of $777$ overcounts certain configurations where three hexagons of the same color are actual neighbors, due to a mistake in the application of the Inclusion-Exclusion Principle. Specifically, when removing the cases where two hexagons have the same color, we have not correctly reflected the removal of those where three or more hexagons share the same color as their neighbors.\n\nThus, upon a corrected consideration, the actual count of valid colorings, considering the properly adjacent requirements, turns out to be $720-12+72=780$. This adjustment accounts for cases where two hexagons share the same color, removing them twice, and adding them in once where in fact they have been entirely excluded by the adjacent non-homogenity requirement, producing a net gain of $72$ unmatched configurations that were omitted from earlier recounting.\n\nTherefore, the total number of ways to color the figure, accounting for the valid configurations excluded initially, is $780$.\n\n$\\boxed{780}$",
