      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to color each of the 12 hexagons either red, yellow, or green in such a way that no two adjacent hexagons have the same color. We can model this problem using graph theory, where each hexagon is a vertex and edges connect adjacent hexagons.\n\nFirst, let's label the hexagons. We can number them as follows: $H_1, H_2, \\ldots, H_{12}$ clockwise. The adjacency relationships between the hexagons are as follows:\n\n- $H_1$ is adjacent to $H_2$ and $H_7$\n- $H_2$ is adjacent to $H_1$ and $H_3$\n- $H_3$ is adjacent to $H_2$ and $H_4$\n- $H_4$ is adjacent to $H_3$ and $H_5$\n- $H_5$ is adjacent to $H_4$ and $H_6$\n- $H_6$ is adjacent to $H_5$ and $H_7$\n- $H_7$ is adjacent to $H_1$ and $H_6$\n\nThus, we can represent the hexagons and their adjacency relationships as a graph with 12 vertices and the mentioned edges. We need to color this graph such that no two adjacent vertices (hexagons) have the same color.\n\nThis problem is equivalent to finding the number of valid vertex-colorings of this cycle graph \\(C_{12}\\) where each vertex is colored either red, yellow, or green. This is a classic problem that can be solved using the chromatic polynomial for a cycle graph.\n\nThe chromatic polynomial for a cycle graph \\(C_n\\) is given by:\n\n\\[ P(C_n, k) = (k-1)^n + (-1)^n(k-1) \\]\n\nwhere \\(k\\) is the number of colors. In our case, \\(n = 12\\) and \\(k = 3\\) (since we have 3 colors: red, yellow, green).\n\nSo, we substitute \\(n = 12\\) and \\(k = 3\\) into the polynomial:\n\n\\[ P(C_{12}, 3) = (3-1)^{12} + (-1)^{12}(3-1) \\]\n\\[ P(C_{12}, 3) = 2^{12} + (-1)^{12} \\cdot 2 \\]\n\\[ P(C_{12}, 3) = 4096 + 2 \\]\n\\[ P(C_{12}, 3) = 4098 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to color the hexagon figure such that no two adjacent hexagons have the same color is \\(\\boxed{4098}\\).",
