    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the number of ways to color the hexagons with a red \\(R\\) such that no two adjacent hexagons have the same color. The key is to notice that the hexagon \\(R\\) is adjacent to five other hexagons (one above, one below, left, and right, and possibly one on the side).\n\n1. **Coloring Hexagon \\(R\\):**\n   - There are 3 choices for the color of the hexagon \\(R\\): red, yellow, or green.\n\n2. **Coloring the Adjacent Hexagons:**\n   - The hexagoins \\(HB\\) and \\(HB_1\\) must have different colors from \\(R\\) and from each other.\n   - The hexagon \\(B_2\\) opposite to \\(R\\) must have a different color from \\(R\\).\n   - The hexagon \\(A_1\\) adjacent to \\(HB\\) and \\(HB_1\\) must have a different color from \\(R\\) and from each other.\n   - The hexagon \\(A_3\\) adjacent to \\(HB_1\\) and \\(HB\\) must have a different color from \\(R\\) and from each other.\n\n3. **Recursive Relation:**\n   - Let \\(a_n\\) be the number of ways to color \\(n\\) hexagons.\n   - For the first hexagon \\(R\\), there are 3 choices.\n   - For the second hexagon \\(H\\), there are 2 choices (it can be the same as \\(R\\) or the same as the adjacent hexagon \\(B_1\\)).\n   - For the third hexagon \\(B\\), there are 2 choices (it can be the same as \\(R\\) or the same as the adjacent hexagon \\(H\\)).\n   - For the fourth hexagon \\(B_1\\), there are 2 choices (it can be the same as \\(H\\) or the same as the adjacent hexagon \\(B_2\\)).\n   - For the fifth hexagon \\(B_2\\), there is 1 choice (it can only be the same as \\(A_1\\)).\n   - For the sixth hexagon \\(A_1\\), there are 2 choices (it can be the same as \\(B_1\\) or the same as the adjacent hexagon \\(B_2\\)).\n   - For the seventh hexagon \\(A_3\\), there are 2 choices (it can be the same as \\(B\\) or the same as the adjacent hexagon \\(B_1\\)).\n\n   Therefore, the number of ways to color \\(n\\) hexagons is:\n   \\[\n   a_n = 3 \\cdot 2^{n-1}\n   \\]\n   For \\(n = 6\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   a_6 = 3 \\cdot 2^5 = 3 \\cdot 32 = 96\n   \\]\n\nThus, the number of different ways to color the figure such that no two adjacent hexagons have the same color is \\(\\boxed{96}\\).",
